    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/ngeo411",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "Previous reconstructions of Last Glacial Maximum ocean temperatures had significant shortcomings, including reliance on single proxies and inconsistent dating, leading to poor constraints for climate models which fail to simulate key observed spatial temperature gradients revealed by multiproxy data.",
    "method": "This paper presents an updated synthesis of sea surface temperatures at the Last Glacial Maximum using a multiproxy approach that integrates microfossil and geochemical reconstructions and includes a quantitative assessment of the reliability of individual records to create a robust global dataset.",
    "task": "The task is to produce an updated global reconstruction of sea surface temperatures during the Last Glacial Maximum by integrating multiple microfossil and geochemical proxy records to constrain the magnitude and patterns of past ocean cooling.",
    "model": [
        "CLIMBER-2",
        "MIROC3.2",
        "CCSM3"
    ],
